## 4. What about AR?

I'd split it into three milestones:

Version 1: Web digital twin. Scan the QR code and explore the room on the phone, with the character inside. No AR needed.

Version 2: AR miniature. Point your phone at the physical model. Use an image marker or tracked model anchor to align a character and animated effects with the physical room. The companion could appear to sit on the bed, walk across the floor or wave through the window.

Version 3: Life-sized portal. Place a life-sized version of the room in your actual environment and walk inside it. This is where Marble's visual environments become especially useful.

For development, I would use Three.js for the web viewer, Gaussian-splat rendering, a separate simplified collision mesh and a real-time conversational agent interface. For precise room-anchored AR, a native ARKit/ARCore or Unity prototype may be more dependable than relying entirely on browser AR.

The harder problems are registration, occlusion and believable character interactions, not simply displaying the splat.

