## 6. What I would manufacture first

OddHobb Tiny Room 001: Your Little World

One universal room shell, approximately 18 × 18 × 18 cm, with interchangeable components.

The first three themes could be:

- The Cosy Bedroom — bed, desk, photographs, rugs, personalised wall art.
- The Tiny Study — bookshelves, computer, desk and miniatures of the owner's interests.
- The Magical Workshop — little alchemical equipment, curiosities, plants, candles and unusual props.

All three use the same room structure and component attachment system.

You could then sell personalised character packs, furniture packs, seasonal decoration packs and later a full house made by stacking modules.

Crucially, I'd source the first production from custom laser-cut panels and JLC-printed furniture rather than trying to modify someone else's copyrighted Rolife design.

The next practical step is one 18 cm universal room CAD file and a BOM, quoted at 1, 25 and 100 units. That gives you a real manufacturing baseline and lets you design both the physical kit and the Marble experience around precisely the same dimensions.

The vision is not merely a customised dollhouse. It's a physical home for a persistent digital character, which its owner can build, expand and inhabit digitally.

This is where OddHobb starts becoming a genuinely interesting hardware platform.

The dollhouse is the physical interface. The robot is the inhabitant. OddHobb is the operating system connecting the physical room, digital world, AI agent and manufacturing network.

And the clever part is that you don't need sophisticated robotics to make the first version compelling.

Imagine a tiny character sitting at a miniature desk. It can turn its head, speak, glow, change facial expressions and respond to you. Its room can change lighting to indicate its state.

When the AI is thinking, the desk lamp pulses. When it's asleep, the room goes dark. When it has something to tell you, the window glows.

You effectively have a physical window into an AI's internal state.

