## 2. The real opportunity is owning the miniature-world standard

Existing evidence: Rolife already sells modular, stackable rooms with lighting and accessories. Its Super Creator rooms use a roughly 16 cm footprint, and its mini series supports figures 7 cm or smaller. That shows the modular format is commercially viable.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.robotimeonline.com\&sz=32)

Robotime

+2



But I wouldn't copy Rolife's proprietary connectors. I'd create an open, documented OddHobb Room Standard.

The standard could specify:

| Interface                | What it enables                              |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| Room footprint           | Stack rooms and connect rooms horizontally   |
| Wall/floor mounting grid | Attach any compatible furniture              |
| Power connector          | Plug-and-play lamps, signs, displays         |
| Data connector           | Connect sensors and motion accessories       |
| Robot dock               | Recharge or power the room's inhabitant      |
| Digital coordinates      | Translate physical layouts into 3D/AR        |
| Asset manifest           | Rebuild, customise and reorder any component |

This becomes the basis of the marketplace.

Someone designs a miniature aquarium, fireplace, floating bookshelf or robot bed. They upload a CAD file, and OddHobb checks its dimensions against the standard.

The customer sees it fitted into their own room, gets a manufacturing quote, clicks buy, and receives the physical part.

Eventually, the room itself has a machine-readable identity:

```
{
  "room": "oddhobb-room-001",
  "scale": "1:24",
  "dimensions_mm": [180, 180, 180],
  "installed": [
    "desk-001",
    "lamp-003",
    "bookshelf-009"
  ],
  "inhabitant": "oddy",
  "capabilities": [
    "light",
    "audio",
    "motion"
  ]
}
```

Your agent knows exactly which objects fit, which are installed, and which upgrades are available.

