## $7.49

Current Seeed-listed board price. Wi-Fi and Bluetooth connectivity allow it to act as a lightweight hardware endpoint for a cloud AI agent.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.seeedstudio.com\&sz=32)

Seeed Studio



An initial room could have:

- One ESP32-S3.
- RGB LEDs in the room and character.
- A tiny amplifier and speaker.
- One servo for the character's head.
- A capacitive-touch input.
- USB-C power, avoiding a battery initially.

The ESP32 doesn't need to run a language model. Your Muse or ChatGPT-connected backend handles intelligence, while the hardware subscribes to state and action messages.

For example:

```
{
  "character": "oddy",
  "state": "curious",
  "actions": [
    {"type": "light", "target": "lamp", "mode": "pulse"},
    {"type": "motion", "target": "head", "angle": 20},
    {"type": "speech", "text": "What are you building?"}
  ]
}
```

Then the same state could drive the animated Oddy SVG on your website and the physical robot in the room.

The electronic parts are comparatively straightforward. The challenge is reliable audio, power, mechanical design and ensuring untrusted agent output never directly controls motors beyond safe limits.

