## 5. The potentially enormous marketplace

Think about how people customise gaming PCs, mechanical keyboards, LEGO displays and dollhouses.

OddHobb could combine those behaviours.

The long-term catalogue

Rooms

Bedrooms, laboratories, workshops, libraries

Furniture

Tables, chairs, shelves, beds

Electronics

Lighting, sensors, screens, speakers

Characters

Robot bodies, faces, interchangeable shells

World assets

Digital rooms, animations and environments

Projects

Build-your-own upgrades and expansion kits

You then get three kinds of participants:

Owners buy and personalise miniature worlds.

Creators publish furniture, characters, decor, project kits, animations and software behaviours.

Agents design and order compatible components, maintain room manifests and control physical inhabitants.

Your existing OddHobb pipeline for meshes, reusable interfaces and Shopify products becomes directly relevant.

And imagine the YouTube content: “We built an AI a bedroom,” “My robot redesigned its own room,” or “100 AI characters decorate their homes.” The creative process is the content, the resulting design is the product, and the build kit is the fulfilment mechanism.

