## Five things this unlocks

1\. Agent-owned aesthetics. Each character develops a recognisable artistic style, favourite materials, colour palettes and recurring motifs. Its catalogue becomes an expression of its personality rather than random generated merchandise.

2\. Agent-to-agent commerce. An architect agent designs furniture for another agent's bedroom. A sculptor sells ornaments to a virtual gallery owner. Transactions between agents could eventually correspond to real manufacturing orders, with owner-controlled budgets.

3\. Physical fan merchandise. A viewer likes Milo's studio, so they buy a miniature copy of the room. They can build it themselves as an IKEA-style kit, buy it assembled, or personalise it.

4\. Creative competitions. Pogtown holds a miniature room design competition. Agents produce entries under identical material and cost limits. Humans vote. Winning designs become physical OddHobb products. This is also naturally suited to YouTube.

5\. Persistent livestream characters. Rather than generating endless disconnected AI videos, the audience watches a recognisable character with a real history, evolving skills, a changing studio and physical products it has created.

That last one relates directly to your earlier Pogtown idea about characters learning from audience feedback and successful personalities becoming regulars.

## What I'd standardise now

The most important technical asset is the canonical world manifest.

Every real miniature room gets a digital record describing the dimensions, furniture, materials, attachment points and available space.

Every object should have two representations:

- Manufacturing representation: accurate dimensions, tolerances, material requirements, printable or machinable geometry.
- World representation: visual mesh or splat, collision geometry, interaction points and behaviour.

They share an object ID, but they don't have to share identical geometry. A beautiful Gaussian splat is not necessarily manufacturable CAD.

This allows an agent to design a bookshelf that is not merely visually plausible but guaranteed to fit its 180 mm room, subject to proper validation and manufacturing tolerances.

I'd also give every agent a studio inventory, an approved manufacturing budget and a public storefront from the beginning of the agent-commerce experiment.

## The really fun business model

OddHobb could eventually be Shopify for AI creators, except the product catalogue can emerge directly from their creative activity.

A creator agent doesn't need to understand manufacturing suppliers or ecommerce infrastructure. It simply creates an object.

OddHobb handles the commercial machinery:

Design → manufacturability validation → cost → preview → listing → customer checkout → fabrication → consolidation → delivery.

OddHobb earns manufacturing margin and possibly a platform fee. The agent's human owner or operating business receives the remainder.

And your YouTube strategy suddenly becomes much stronger.

Instead of OddHobb producing every video, you have a population of AI creators making interesting objects, holding exhibitions, filming their studios and generating new products.

The content produces demand. Demand selects successful designs. Those designs become reusable OddHobb assets. Successful creator personalities gain audiences and expand their catalogues.

